// Georgia is an EXTERNAL-STUDIES (EKSTERNATI) TIER market — the
// Caucasus sibling of Azerbaijan's supervised model.
// - The Law of Georgia on General Education (2005) governs. The
//   Constitution (Art. 27 § 2) makes PRIMARY AND BASIC education
//   compulsory.
// - CRITICAL 2024 CHANGE — STATE IT PROMINENTLY, IT IS A
//   DIFFERENTIATOR: amendments of 18 July 2024 extended basic
//   education from three years to four. The structure is now
//   primary (grades I-VI), basic (grades VII-X), secondary
//   (grades XI-XII) — and compulsory education therefore runs
//   THROUGH GRADE TEN, to about AGE SIXTEEN. Most pages online
//   still cite the old nine-grade rule; we cite the new one.
// - Parental-choice "homeschooling" is not an established named
//   right. The lawful mechanism is the EKSTERNATI (external
//   studies) form: a person may master the general education
//   programme independently and validate it through written
//   examinations organized by the Ministry — basic and secondary
//   stage exams at schools defined by the Ministry's territorial
//   bodies, with resource centres able to conduct the final exam of
//   complete general education, and a formal appeals committee.
//   State this exactly; arrangements run through the Ministry's
//   territorial bodies; we help families document and prepare.
//   Expect the state track in Georgian — plan Georgian-language
//   preparation all year (the Hungary/Azerbaijan dual-track
//   discipline).
// - THE WINDOW: secondary education (grades XI-XII) is NOT
//   compulsory. After grade ten, at about sixteen, the A-Level
//   years run at the family's choice. Post-2024 the window sits at
//   sixteen, not fifteen — never cite the old boundary.
// - The safe configurations, as everywhere: supplementary beside an
//   enrolment (always clean), the eksternati route for families
//   completing externally, and the post-grade-10 window.
// MARKET NOTE: Tbilisi carries the tier — the New School, QSI
// Tbilisi, the European School, the British-track schools, and the
// British Council — at roughly USD 5,000-20,000, serving a capital
// transformed by the remote-work and relocation wave of recent
// years (professionals and remote workers from across the region
// and beyond — keep framing NEUTRAL and non-political). Batumi
// booms with almost no provision; Kutaisi holds an English-taught
